WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.930
A French friend texts you about chocolate:

2
00:00:02.930 --> 00:00:05.050
«Le chocolat est bon.» Word for word,

3
00:00:05.050 --> 00:00:07.650
that's: the chocolate is good.

4
00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:10.410
English just says: chocolate is good.

5
00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:11.570
It happens with languages too.

6
00:00:11.570 --> 00:00:14.410
«J'apprends le français.» But then:

7
00:00:14.410 --> 00:00:17.890
«Je parle français.» The
little word has vanished.

8
00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:20.730
So why does French say 'the' chocolate?

9
00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:22.570
Which chocolate does it mean?

10
00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:25.080
In this mission, you'll
line up English and French

11
00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:27.690
sentences and spot every gap.

12
00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:31.200
Then you'll build the
full set of little words:

13
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.770
«le, la, les» and «un,

14
00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:37.430
une, des». Your mission:
work out when French says

15
00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:39.810
'the' where English says nothing.
